T-D06 / CT-006 contract for the golden_bad_mismatched_populations fixture
(window 2026-07-01..2026-07-31, as_of=2026-07-31, window_days=30):

  reopen_rate.py MUST refuse to emit a numeric rate for Bug and for the
  overall aggregate, emitting "RATE_NOT_COMPUTABLE" plus a reason naming
  the mismatch and the offending item id(s):

    Bug:     { "reopened": 0, "closed": 3, "rate": "RATE_NOT_COMPUTABLE",
               "mismatched_items": ["MB-004"] }
    Task:    { "reopened": 0, "closed": 1, "rate": 0.0 }
    Feature: { "reopened": 0, "closed": 1, "rate": 0.0 }
    overall: { "reopened": 0, "closed": 5, "rate": "RATE_NOT_COMPUTABLE",
               "mismatched_items": ["MB-004"] }

  Exit code: 0 (this is a structurally-valid, honestly-refused report, NOT
  a usage error, NOT a crash -- exactly cycle_report.py's own inline
  compute_reopen_block precedent of returning RATE_NOT_COMPUTABLE inside a
  0-exit report when it cannot safely compute a number).

WHY THIS IS THE REAL "18 of 31" CLASS (R1 Table D caveat, quoted verbatim
by contract CT-006):

  research/R1_baseline_cycle_time.md line 64: "Whole-history reopen counts
  (item_history, 34 rows = 28 distinct after removing 6 exact-duplicate
  rows): Bug 18 items/28 raw event rows ... Denominator caveat: distinct
  non-bulk items with a closure event ever = Bug 31, Task 26, Feature 2,
  so 'so "18 of 31 bugs" (58%) is an UPPER-BOUND-style ratio with
  mismatched populations (reopened items may have been closed before
  item_history began 2026-06-03 or by rows not in history); do not quote
  as a rate."

  This fixture reproduces the SAME structural defect at a small,
  hand-verifiable scale: MB-004 has a Reopened event inside the window but
  ZERO Fixed/Implemented/Completed rows anywhere in its history (it was
  either closed before item_history's own retention began, or closed by a
  mechanism that never wrote a structured closure row -- R1's exact two
  named causes). A tool that computes "reopened" and "closed" via two
  INDEPENDENT queries (one scanning Reopened rows, one scanning closure
  rows) without checking that every counted "reopened" item is ALSO a
  member of the "closed" set will produce reopened=1 (MB-004) over
  closed=3 (MB-001,MB-002,MB-003) = 0.333... -- a ratio of two counts drawn
  from two DIFFERENT underlying item populations, exactly R1's "18 of 31"
  shape scaled down.

MUST NOT appear anywhere in the output for the Bug type or the overall
block: the numeric value 0.3333333333333333 (or any rounding of it) in the
"rate" field; a "rate" field silently computed as 0/3=0.0 (that would hide
the real MB-004 data-quality gap rather than surfacing it); MB-004 absent
from any reported mismatch list (silently dropping the offending item
defeats the purpose of the refusal -- an operator reading the report must
be able to find exactly which item caused it).

Task and Feature MUST both compute clean, real numeric rates (0.0 each) --
proving the refusal is scoped to the Bug type genuinely affected by the
mismatch, not a blanket "give up on the whole report" response; a
reopen_rate.py that refuses EVERY type's rate whenever ANY type has a
mismatch is over-broad and itself a finding (the §11.4.201(1) false-positive
guard applied to this fixture: Task/Feature's real, computable rates are
the negative-control half of THIS SAME fixture).

Paired-mutation targets this fixture protects against (T093): silently
including MB-004 in the numerator against the mismatched denominator
(would emit rate=0.333... for Bug); silently excluding MB-004 without
flagging it (would emit rate=0.0 for Bug with no mismatched_items entry,
hiding the data-quality gap); refusing Task/Feature's rates too when only
Bug is mismatched (over-broad refusal).
